1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,007
A luggage scanner at the harbor gate pings
your Boot process: GET /luggage/TAG-8841.
Tomcat accepted the socket. Something
inside the JVM still has to decide which
Java method owns that tag, call it, and
write JSON back.

2
00:00:16,128 --> 00:00:29,519
That center of gravity is not "Spring
magic." It is DispatcherServlet. Harbor
teams used to register a servlet per
concern — one for luggage tags, one for
berth boards, one for customs forms.

3
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:38,448
Each copy-pasted encoding, error pages,
and content-type negotiation. Change the
error shape in luggage and forget customs.

4
00:00:38,567 --> 00:00:51,192
The front-controller pattern collapses
that duplication: one servlet owns the
HTTP pipeline; handlers answer one kind of
request. In a Boot web app you rarely
declare DispatcherServlet by hand.

5
00:00:51,311 --> 00:01:02,831
DispatcherServletAutoConfiguration
registers it, usually mapped to /.
Underneath it remains a servlet. Every
request that reaches it runs doDispatch.

6
00:01:02,951 --> 00:01:20,304
If you can narrate doDispatch, you can
debug a 404 without a slide deck. Walk GET
/luggage/TAG-8841 through the chain. The
HTTP request arrives at the servlet
container. The container hands it to
DispatcherServlet.

7
00:01:20,424 --> 00:01:32,904
The servlet asks a HandlerMapping: given
this URL, verb, and headers, which handler
should run? The mapping returns a
HandlerExecutionChain — typically a
controller method plus any interceptors.

8
00:01:33,024 --> 00:01:42,623
Next the servlet asks a HandlerAdapter:
can you invoke this kind of handler?
Annotated controllers are not called by
raw reflection from the servlet.

9
00:01:42,743 --> 00:01:57,144
An adapter binds parameters, invokes the
method, and wraps the return value. The
adapter calls the controller. The
controller returns a body — for luggage, a
tag status payload — and the servlet
finishes the response.

10
00:01:57,263 --> 00:02:08,448
The client sees status, headers, and
bytes. Say the pipeline once without
ornament: HTTP request → DispatcherServlet
→ HandlerMapping → HandlerAdapter →
controller → response.

11
00:02:08,567 --> 00:02:12,743
If you keep only one diagram from this
lesson, keep that.

12
00:02:12,863 --> 00:02:22,415
Here is the handler the mapping will find
for that luggage tag — ordinary Spring MVC
code, not a call you make into doDispatch
yourself: Look at this java for a moment.

13
00:02:22,535 --> 00:02:32,567
At startup, a RequestMappingHandlerMapping
reads @GetMapping("/{tag}") and records
that GET /luggage/TAG-8841 belongs to
byTag.

14
00:02:32,687 --> 00:02:39,623
At request time, DispatcherServlet asks
that mapping “which handler?” and gets
back this method (plus interceptors).

15
00:02:39,743 --> 00:02:54,863
Separately, a RequestMappingHandlerAdapter
answers “how do I call it?” — bind {tag}
from the path, invoke byTag, write the
returned LuggageStatus as JSON via message
conversion. Mapping chooses; adapting
invokes.

16
00:02:54,983 --> 00:03:04,727
That split is why the same servlet can
drive a Thymeleaf berth board and this
luggage JSON endpoint without becoming a
god class. Look at this java for a moment.

17
00:03:04,847 --> 00:03:19,871
Operationally, Boot’s default is one
dispatcher for the app. Filters still sit
outside this story on the servlet filter
chain. Interceptors sit inside the handler
execution chain. Those layers get their
own lessons.

18
00:03:19,991 --> 00:03:26,375
The center holds either way: almost every
Spring MVC request you care about passes
through doDispatch.

19
00:03:26,495 --> 00:03:40,583
People sometimes treat DispatcherServlet
as the thing that wires collaborators or
as a synonym for the application context.
Wrong layer. The servlet uses the web
application context to find mappings and
adapters.

20
00:03:40,703 --> 00:03:50,711
It does not assemble your luggage lookup
graph. Another trap: assuming every
controller annotation is interpreted at
request time by the servlet itself.

21
00:03:50,831 --> 00:03:58,319
Mapping metadata is read at startup into
handler mappings; invocation is delegated
to an adapter when traffic arrives.

22
00:03:58,439 --> 00:04:08,975
Memorizing "Boot auto-configures a
dispatcher" will not help you when GET
/luggage/TAG-8841 returns 404 — that
usually means no HandlerMapping claimed
the path.

23
00:04:09,095 --> 00:04:18,455
A 415 or binding failure usually means the
adapter and converters disagreed with the
client. Those failures live on this
pipeline.

24
00:04:18,575 --> 00:04:25,007
We named the front door and watched one
luggage GET move through mapping and
adaptation into LuggageTagController.

25
00:04:25,127 --> 00:04:35,879
Controllers are no longer a mystery shape
— but the annotations that claim verbs,
paths, and payloads still deserve their
own slow look: how @GetMapping and
@PathVariable become mapping metadata,

26
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,480
what happens when two methods compete for
the same pattern, and how a return type
becomes a status and a body.

27
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:45,648
That is where controller design starts.
